vance to Huntington's disease. <source>J. Cell Sci.</source> <volume>115</volume>:<fpage>941</fpage>&#x02013;948.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11870213</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib11"><citation citation-type="journal">Humbert, S., E.A. Bryson, F.P. Cordelieres, N.C. Connors, S.R. Datta, S. Finkbeiner, M.E. Greenberg, and F. Saudou. <year>2002</year>. The IGF-1/Akt pathway is neuroprotective in Huntington's disease and involves Huntingtin phosphorylation by Akt. <source>Dev. Cell.</source> <volume>2</volume>:<fpage>831</fpage>&#x02013;837.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12062094</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib12"><citation citation-type="journal">Ino, H., T. Ishizuka, T. Chiba, and M. Tatibana. <year>1994</year>. Expression of CDK5 (PSSALRE kinase), a neural cdc2-related protein kinase, in the mature and developing mouse central and peripheral nervous systems. <source>Brain Res.</source> <volume>661</volume>:<fpage>196</fpage>&#x02013;206.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7834371</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><citation citation-type="journal">Kim, Y.J., Y. Yi, E. Sapp, Y. Wang, B. Cuiffo, K.B. Kegel, Z.H. Qin, N. Aronin, and M. DiFiglia. <year>2001</year>. Caspase 3-cleaved N-terminal fragments of wild-type and mutant huntingtin are present in normal and Huntington's disease brains, associate with membranes, and undergo calpain-dependent proteolysis. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>98</volume>:<fpage>12784</fpage>&#x02013;12789.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11675509</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><citation citation-type="journal">LaFevre-Bernt, M.A., and L.M. Ellerby. <year>2003</year>. Kennedy's disease. Phosphorylation of the polyglutamine-expanded form of androgen receptor regulates its cleavage by caspase-3 and enhances cell death. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>278</volume>:<fpage>34918</fpage>&#x02013;34924.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12824190</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><citation citation-type="journa